individual's perceptions. Re-educative approaches to therapy are directed toward
producing more harmonious self-structure. Rogers' client-centered approach is an
outstanding example of insight therapy with rc-educative goals (Tucker, 1970).
The reconstructive approach to therapy has for its objective the gaining of insight
into an individual's unconscious conflicts, thereby bringing about extensive alteration
in the individual's character structure and the release of energies for the development
of new adaptive capacities. Freudian and other psychoanalytic approaches to
psychotherapy adumbrate this point of view.
