Psychoanalysis was found to have varying degrees of success with different ^pes
of clients plagued by different kinds of psychological ailments. Counselling is
concerned with clients who have problems that do not compare by any stretch of
imagination with those of psychoneurosis or psychosis. Most of the counselling
problems are simple, mild to moderately disturbing and are usually of a brief
duration. The psychoanalytic technique, as pracUced, requires enormous time and
most counselling clients cannot spare that much time for therapy. Due to such
practical reasons the psychoanalytic approach is not usually employed in coimselling
situations. We shall examine the alternative approaches one by one.
Some of the major criticisms levelled against psychoanalysis are the following:
1. The deterministic view of man portrays him as a nasty person drivOT
relentlessly by animalistic insdncts, unconscious needs and repressed urges.
2. Freud's system implies the dualism of the body and mind as a distinct
phenomena which emphasizes psychosomatic phenomena or 'reaction
formations'.
3. Too much emphasis is placed upon childhood experiences and the present
maladjustment is sought to be explained on the basis of some experience in
early childhood. This would make the individual feel helpless and incapable
of overcoming his difficulties.
4. In Freud's system all behaviour is determined by the psychic energy which
could flow into or towards one object or another. This explanation minimizes
the importance of situational events.
5. Freud minimizes the role of rationality in human behaviour. But in ther^y
Freud £q)pears to unwittingly take recourse to rationality. In psychoanalysis
the repressed material is brought out and interpreted for the client and this
makes him understand his irrational fears, complexes, etc., that is. the
individual becomes more knowledgeable.


