In the course of development, the child differentiates himself from the rest of his
environment so that a part of his experience is symbolized in his awareness as self-
experience. This leads to the concept of the self becoming defined through interaction
with others of significance in the environment. The child perceives experience as
'"selity. For him, his experiences are either satisfying or not satisfying. They are,
thCTefore, valued as positive or negative. The negatively valued experiences are
avoided while the positive ones are sought All such experiences are integrated into
the growing self-concept of the individual. The child desires regard from others.
The satisfaction of this need for regard depends upon his situation and other
individuals. The need for regard is reciprocal, that is, the positive regard of a
significant number of members can lead to a positive regard of one's self. The need
for self-regard develops from the experiences that grow out of association with
others. From the sense of self-regard develops a condition of self-worth. The need
for positive regard from others grows and develops into a need in itself. The need
for self-regard leads to the selective perception of experiences and thereby reality.
Experiences which are congruent and in accord with one's conditions of worth are
perceived and are integrated into awareness, while those incongruent or discordant
with one's conceptions of self-worth are distorted, that is, they are not perceived
as they 'are* but as they 'are desired to be'. The experiences thus get distorted and
are denied to awareness. They are not integrated and organized into the self-structure.


