Group counselling ordinarily involves five stages, namely (1) the formation of the
group, (2) the involvement stage, (3) the transition stage, (4) the working stage and
(5) the ending stage.
Group counselling programmes are organized to cater to a wide variety of needs.
The objective is of primary importance in organizing any group for this programme.
For what purpose is the group being organized? This will largely and broadly
determine the otha* steps in the formation of the group.
Selection of members—This is perhaps the most crucial step. As stated earlier,
the members of a group have common goals and are largely homogeneous. While
homogeneous grouping in relation to age and maturity is viewed as desirable,
comprising the group slightly differently has often been found to yield desirable
results. Therefore, the groups can comprise individuals of different age levels or of
different sexes. There are certain basic guidelines in selecting individuals who
comprise a group. Individual counselling is recommended very often as a preliminary
step in the selection of members for a group. This step was found to help prevent
individuals from withdrawing during the group counselling sessions. It should be
noted that the very aggressive, the very shy and the seriously maladjusted cannot
be put into the same group. The previous relationship of the members should also
be considered; close friends and relatives should not be in the same group.
Size ofthe group—Opinions differ concerning the exact number in a group. Six
is the number suggested by some, while others are prepared to go as far as twelve?
What has to be borne in mind is that, as the group increases in size, collateral
relationships are often weakened. The tendency to depend on the counsellor rather
than upon one another becomes increasingly evident. Frequently, when the size of
the group increases, the counsellor is found to talk more to the group as a whole
rather than to the members as individuals.
Frequency of sessions—Some recommend weekly sessions while others two
sessions per week. More fequent sessions may not be productive because the
individuals would have no chance to think over the experience of previous sessions.
Duration of sessions—This Obviously depends on several factors. Children cannot
be engaged fruitfully for sessions longer than of 30 minutes duration. But with
adults the sessions can be longer—usually ofan hour and a half duration. However,


